  "body": "## Summary\n\n- Replace `-comp gzip` with `-noI -noD -noF -noX` (all squashfs compression disabled).\n- Addresses slow AppImage cold-launch (multi-minute startup) for large Qt apps like Logos Basecamp.\n\n## Why\n\nThe AppImage type-2 runtime FUSE-mounts the embedded squashfs and reads blocks on every page fault during app startup. For a Qt app that `dlopen`s many frameworks, QML plugins, and module `.so`s, cold startup reads thousands of distinct blocks through that FUSE layer \u2014 and any compressor (gzip, zstd, etc.) means each read pays CPU decompression cost.\n\nFor Logos Basecamp this stretches cold launch into minutes, compounded by a downstream 1-second token-socket handshake window in liblogos: if child module processes take >1 s to reach their `QLocalServer::listen()` call (because their binary + Qt deps are being decompressed through FUSE for the first time), the parent kills them. Every subsequent IPC call then times out at 20 s per request, which is where the user-visible freezing comes from.\n\nLocal Nix runs and macOS `.app` bundles (plain directories, no compression layer) don't see this at all, which matches the reported symptom.\n\nTrading some on-disk size for near-native launch performance is the right call: the common case (users launching the app) should not pay a perceptible cost, even if the AppImage is larger to distribute.\n\n`squashfuse_ll` (used by the pinned `type2-runtime`) handles uncompressed squashfs transparently \u2014 no runtime compatibility concerns.\n\n## Test plan\n\n- [ ] Build a Logos Basecamp AppImage with this change and measure cold startup time vs. main\n- [ ] Confirm embedded modules (capability_module, package_manager) now load without token-socket timeouts\n- [ ] Compare AppImage size vs. gzip baseline (expect ~2-3x larger, acceptable)\n- [ ] Verify AppImage still executes via FUSE mount (no fallback to `--appimage-extract-and-run`)\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
